    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 091711\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion \nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n1010 AM PDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through this \n  afternoon, with dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially \n  widespread precipitation arrive late Saturday through through \n  Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the middle of next week.\n\nHigh pressure remains in place through this afternoon before \nweakening ahead of a trough that digs over the Pacific Northwest. \nThis will lead to one more day of Major Heat Risk in Death Valley, \nwhere an Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Friday \nevening. Elsewhere, minor heat risk is expected, with a few areas of \nmoderate risk across the Colorado River Valley and lower desert \nelevations. Minor Heat Risk for most areas Saturday, but that will \nlower into the Little to No Risk category by Sunday as much cooler \ntemperatures are expected. \n\nOtherwise, we continue to monitor the phasing of tropical storm \nRachel with an incoming trough out of the Pacific Northwest. \nOverall, there have generally been some minor changes in the track \nover the past 12 hours as it pulls northeast into the region and it \nstill looks like the greatest threat of widespread precipitation \nwill be across western Arizona east through Lincoln, Clark, and \neastern San Bernardino Counties. The models have remained fairly \nconsistent that areas across Mohave County could see the highest \nprecipitation amounts, generally ranging from 1.00-2.00 inches \nstarting late Saturday night and continuing through Sunday night \nbefore decreasing. Areas to the east across southern Nevada and \neastern San Bernardino County have the potential of seeing 0.50-1.00 \ninch by Monday morning, with over an inch possible in the Spring \nMountains. Again, any slight shift in the track of Rachel and the \ndeepening west coast trough could bring changes to the overall \nprecipitation amounts. Shower and thunderstorm chances look to \npersist through Tuesday as a closed low tracks across southern \nNevada. This will bring snow levels down to around 9,000 feet, so it \nis quite possible that the Spring Mountains could see a little snow \non the peaks. In addition, there will also be a concern for stronger \nsurface winds near the remnant circulation as it moves across the \nlower Colorado River Valley and western Arizona, where some wind \ngusts of 40-50 mph or greater will be possible. A Wind Advisory has \nbeen issued for portions of Inyo County in California, and Nye and \nLincoln counties in Nevada starting Saturday afternoon and \ncontinuing through Sunday night. It is likely that additional wind \nadvisories will be needed later in the weekend. \n\nIn addition, much cooler temperatures will follow for Monday and \nTuesday as the low moves through, with heights falling below 560dm. \nHeights will slowly climb later in the week as high pressure \nrebuilds and temperatures return closer to normal. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill become southeast this afternoon. There is a 60%chance for wind \nspeeds over 10KT this afternoon. Isolated gusts 15-20KT are possible \nat times after 21Z. Winds will subside after sunset with southwest \nwinds at or below 8KT expected overnight. On Saturday expect \nincreasing south winds. Gusts to around 20KT are expected Saturday \nmorning before increasing closer to 30KT for the afternoon. VFR and \ndry conditions are likely through the night. Increase clouds around \n12kft are expected on Saturday, especially in the afternoon. There \nis a low chance (20%) for scattered showers and thunderstorms after \n21Z Saturday, but confidence is too low to include mentions of \nprecipitation in the TAF at this time.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow \ntypical daily directional patterns with speeds of 10KT or less for \nmost locations today. The exception will be in the lower Colorado \nRiver Valley where enhanced south winds gusting 15-20KT are expected \nthis afternoon. Winds will subside this evening with light winds \nunder 10KT expected through tonight. On Saturday, increasing south \nto southeast winds are expected across the region. Widespread gust \n20-30KT are expected with higher gusts over 35KT in the Southern \nGreat Basin and Inyo County. It should remain dry through Saturday \nmorning with VFR conditions as increasing clouds at 10kft-15kft are \nexpected late in the period.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE....Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas      97(2024)*\nBishop         93(2015)\nNeedles       104(1996)*\nDaggett       103(1980)\nKingman        98(1910)\nDesert Rock    97(1996)\nDeath Valley  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     72(2024)*\nBishop        61(2025)\nNeedles       76(1988)*\nDaggett       69(1996)*\nKingman       68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Nickerson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
